analysisWe need to read the comment: "@@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then if the object is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index equals the object."

We need to produce an assert statement that captures this specification.

We are in method add(int index, E obj). The method adds a new element at given index (before current element at that index). The comment says: If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then if the object is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index equals the object.

But after insertion, the element at that index should be the inserted object (obj). So the spec: if 0 <= index <= \old(this.size()) (initial size) then after operation, the element at that index equals obj (or both null). Actually they phrase: "if the object is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index equals the object." That's essentially: this.get(index) == null when obj == null, else this.get(index).equals(obj). So we can express: (obj == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(obj))

We also need to include premise: index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()) . Use =>.

Thus assert: (index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => (obj == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(obj));

